    references: ["saiki1988", "sigma-pcr", "duewer2018"],
    concept: "The polymerase chain reaction copies the stretch of DNA between two primers. Each cycle has three steps: heat to separate the strands (denaturation), cool so the primers bind their sites (annealing, typically about 5 \xB0C below the primers\u2019 melting temperature), then let a heat-stable polymerase such as Taq extend them at 72 \xB0C. The primers point toward each other on opposite strands, so every new strand carries the other primer\u2019s site and becomes a template in the next cycle: in an ideal cycle the target doubles. The first cycles make strands of variable length; exact-length copies of the amplicon first appear in the third cycle and soon dominate, so after n ideal cycles one template gives about 2\u207F copies. Here 3.75 ng of human genomic DNA, at about 3.3 pg per haploid genome, is about 1,100 starting copies. Real reactions fall short of perfect doubling and eventually level off in a plateau phase.",
